                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+algebroid"} ∞-Lie algebroid
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+symplectic+geometry"} higher symplectic geometry
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Klein+geometry"} higher Klein geometry
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Cartan+geometry"} higher Cartan geometry
                  %p
                    %strong Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"}> Jones' theorem
                        ,
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Deligne-Kontsevich conjecture
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality+for+geometric+stacks"} Tannaka duality for geometric stacks
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#closure_properties"} Closure properties
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#classes_of_examples"} Classes of examples
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#AsLocallyConstantSheaves"} As locally constant sheaves
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em étale morphism of schemes
            is the realization of the general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+morphism"} étale morphism
            for maps between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+homeomorphism"} local homeomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphism"} local diffeomorphism
          %p
            A central use of étale morphisms of schemes is that they appear as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverings"} coverings
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+site"} étale site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
            with respect to these étale covers of schemes is accordingly called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cohomology"} étale cohomology
          %h2#definition Definition
          #EquivalentConditionsForEtale.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
              is an
              %strong étale morphism
              if the following equivalent conditions hold:
            %ol
              %li
                %p it is
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+morphism+of+schemes"} smooth
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unramified+morphism"} unramified
              %li
                %p it is
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+morphism+of+schemes"} smooth
                  %li
                    %p
                      of
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+dimension"} relative dimension
                      = succeed "." do
                        %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                          %semantics
                            %mrow
                              %mn 0
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %li
                %p it is
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+morphism"} flat
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unramified"}> unramified
                      ;
              %li
                %p it is
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"}> formally étale
                      ;
                  %li
                    %p
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+of+finite+presentation"} locally of finite presentation
          %p
            (A number of other equivalent definitions are listed at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etale_morphism"} wikipedia
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              For morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \longrightarrow Y
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+varieties"} algebraic varieties
              over an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
              this means that for all points
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in X
              the induced morphism on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+cones"} tangent cones
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi p
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi p
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    T_p X \longrightarrow T_{f(p)} Y
            %p
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphisms"} local diffeomorphisms
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Relaxing the finiteness condition in item 4 of
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquivalentConditionsForEtale"}
              yields the notion of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} weakly étale morphism
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"} étale morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"} pro-étale morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"} weakly étale morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+schemes"} formally étale morphism
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A jointly surjective collection of étale morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
              is called an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cover"} étale cover
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Most of the pairs of conditions in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EquivalentConditionsForEtale"}
              can be read as constraining the fiber of the morphism to be first suitably surjective/
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"}> bundle
              \-like (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+morphism+of+schemes"}> smooth
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+morphism"} flat
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unramified"} unramified
            %p
              Specifically the first condition has an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
              analog: a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} formally étale morphism
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+smooth+morphism"} formally smooth
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+unramified+morphism"} formally unramified morphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
              and there they correspond to the statement that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+diffeomorphism"} local diffeomorphism
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersion"} submersion
              which is also an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/immersion+of+smooth+manifolds"} immersion of smooth manifolds
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A morphism is
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} formally étale morphism
              if it is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+smooth"} formally smooth
                  (satisfying an infinitesimal lifting property)
              %li
                %p
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+unramified"} formally unramified
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              These are sheaf-like properties, which can be formalized in the language of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Q-categories"} Q-categories
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopresheaf"} monopresheaf
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epipresheaf"} epipresheaf
              properties on the
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %p
              See at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
              and at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#closure_properties Closure properties
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  A
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite"} composite
                  of two étale morphism is itself étale.
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  of an étale morphism is étale.
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1 \circ f_2
                  is étale and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1
                  is, then so is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi f
                            %mn 2
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Milne"} Milne, prop. 2.11
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Use that an étale morphism is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} formally étale morphism
              with finite fibers, and that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
              is formally étale precisely if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal shape modality
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+a+monad"} unit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} naturality square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Π
                              %mi inf
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Π
                              %mi inf
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \Pi_{inf}(X)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    Y &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \Pi_{inf}(Y)
                    }
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              square. Then the three properties to be shown are equivalently the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
              for pullback diagrams.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+morphism+of+schemes"} smooth morphism of schemes
              is étale iff there is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cover"} étale cover
              of the base scheme by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subschemes"} open subschemes
              such that the pullback of the projection to each of them is an open local isomorphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+ringed+spaces"} locally ringed spaces
              (and in particular, the pullback of the projection morphism is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tal%C3%A9+space"} étale map
              of the corresponding underlying topological spaces).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              This disjointness picture of étale covers make them suitable for having nontrivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              in situations where Zariski covers give vanishing cohomology.
          %h3#classes_of_examples Classes of examples
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to Spec k
              is étale precisely if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %mi Spec
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \simeq \coprod_i Spec k_i
              for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_i
              a finite and separable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+extension"} field extension
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            This appears for instance as
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#deJong"} de Jong, prop. 3.1 i)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              Such étale morphisms are classified by the classical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
              of field extensions.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              homomorphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+varieties"} affine varieties
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(A) \to Spec(B)
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(B)
              non-singular and for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \simeq B[x_1, \cdots, x_n]/(f_1, \cdots, f_n)
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
              is étale precisel if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobian"} Jacobian
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi det
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} det(\frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x_j})
              is invertible.
          %p
            This appears for instance as (
            %a{:href => "#Milne"}> Milne, prop. 2.1
            ).
          %h3#AsLocallyConstantSheaves As locally constant sheaves
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              on a scheme
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              corresponds to an étale morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to X
              precisely if there is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cover"} étale cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \to X\}
              such that each restriction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi LConst
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F|_{U_i} \simeq LConst K_i
            %p
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphic"} isomorphic
              to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+sheaf"} constant sheaf
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi K
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_i
          %p
            A proof is in (
            %a{:href => "#Deligne"}> Deligne
            ).
          %h2#Examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              A finite separable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+extension"} field extension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \hookrightarrow L
              corresponds dually to an étale morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Spec
                      %mi L
                      %mo →
                      %mi Spec
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec L \to Spec K
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+theory"} Galois theory
          #OpenImmersionIsEtale.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+immersion+of+schemes"} open immersion of schemes
              is an étale morphism of schemes. In particular a standard open inclusion (a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
              in the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+a+commutative+ring"} localization of a commutative ring
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Spec(R[S^{-1}]) \longrightarrow Spec(R)
            %p is étale.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Stacks+Project"} Stacks Project, lemma 28.37.9
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              By one of the equivalent characterizations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+morphism"} étale morphism
              it is sufficient to check that the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R[S^{-1}]) \longrightarrow Spec(R)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+%C3%A9tale+morphism"} formally étale morphism
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+of+finite+presentation"} locally of finite presentation
            %p The latter is clear, since the very definition of
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msubsup
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi s
                      %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                    %msubsup
                      %mi s
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi n
                    %msubsup
                      %mi s
                      %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R[S^{-1}] = R[s_1^{-1}, \cdots, s_n^{-1}](s_1 s_1^{-1} - 1, \cdots , s_n s_n^{-1} - 1)
            %p
              exhibits a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+presented+algebra"} finitely presented algebra
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %p
              To see that it is formally étale we need to check that for every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideal"} nilpotent ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ,
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi R
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ,
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi J
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi R
                            %mo ,
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Hom
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi R
                            %mo ,
                            %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi J
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Hom(R[S^{-1}], T) &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Hom(R[S^{-1}],T/J)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    Hom(R, T) &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Hom(R, T/J)
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+a+commutative+ring"} localization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[S^{-1}] \longrightarrow T
              is a homomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \longrightarrow T
              which sends all elements in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow R
              to invertible elements in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideal"} nilpotent ideal
              can be invertible, Therefore the fiber product of the bottom and right map is the set of maps from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is taken to invertibles, which is indeed the top left set.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+morphism"} étale morphism
            %li
              %p
                %strong étale morphism of schemes
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+site"}> étale site
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+cohomology"} étale cohomology
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-site"} étale (∞,1)-site
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+morphism+of+E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} étale morphism of E-∞ rings
          %h2#references References
          %p The classical references are
          %ul#Deligne
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"} Pierre Deligne
              et al.,
              %em Cohomologie étale
              , Lecture Notes in Mathematics, no. 569, Springer-Verlag, 1977.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Milne"}> James Milne
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89tale+Cohomology"} Étale Cohomology
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 33
          %p Lecture notes include
          %ul#Milne
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Milne"}> James Milne
              , section 2 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+%C3%89tale+Cohomology"} Lectures on Étale Cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aise+Johan+de+Jong"}> Aise Johan de Jong
              ,
              %em Étale cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.columbia.edu/~pugin/Teaching/Etale_files/EtaleCohomology.pdf"}> pdf
              )
              %p#deJong — link broken, couldn’t find another copy online
          %p The local structure theorems are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Leovigildo Alonso, Ana Jeremias, Marta Perez,
              %em Local structure theorems for smooth maps of formal schemes
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605115"}> arXiv:math/0605115
              )
          %p
            Discussion of etale morphisms between
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-infinity+rings"} E-infinity rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+schemes"} spectral schemes
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 8.5 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
          %p
            and generally in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+geometry"} E-∞ geometry
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 1.2 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quasi-Coherent+Sheaves+and+Tannaka+Duality+Theorems"} Quasi-Coherent Sheaves and Tannaka Duality Theorems
        .revisedby
